/* OCR A level Geography (H481) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'OCR-H481', KITS: {"1.1.1":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.1.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Coastal landscapes is the compulsory entry point into Topic 1.1 for centres choosing Option A, and it underpins every higher-tariff question on Papers 01 and 03: examiners expect systems thinking (inputs, processes, outputs, sediment cells), named case-study evidence for high and low energy coastlines, and an evaluative grip on how physical processes and human intervention interact to change the coast, from a single storm to millennia of sea level change.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Coastal landscapes as systems","t":"A coastline can be modelled as an open system: energy (wave, wind, tidal) and material (sediment) enter as inputs, are transformed by geomorphic processes such as erosion, transport and deposition, and leave as outputs when sediment exits a sediment cell or energy is dissipated on the shore. Examiners reward candidates who frame answers around this input-process-output structure rather than listing landforms in isolation. A top-level answer explicitly uses systems vocabulary - inputs, stores, flows, sediment cell, dynamic equilibrium - to explain why a coastline behaves the way it does, rather than simply describing what is there."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Physical factors: wind, waves, tides, geology and currents","t":"Wind speed, duration and fetch determine wave height and energy; wave type (constructive versus destructive) then controls whether a beach builds up or is stripped back. Tidal range widens or narrows the zone over which wave energy acts each day, while geology - rock type (lithology) and the arrangement of rock layers (structure, including dip and jointing) - governs how fast and in what shapes a coast erodes. Global ocean currents redistribute heat and can influence the storms reaching a coast. A top-level answer links two or more of these factors together, for example showing how a long fetch combined with steeply dipping weak rock produces rapid headland retreat, rather than treating each factor separately."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Sources of coastal sediment","t":"Sediment reaching the coast comes from terrestrial sources (rivers depositing fluvial sediment, weathering and mass movement supplying cliff material, marine erosion of the coast itself, wind-blown aeolian deposition, and longshore drift moving sediment along the shore), from offshore marine deposition, and from human sources such as beach nourishment. A top-level answer treats these sources as the inputs of the sediment budget for a named cell, and uses them to explain whether that cell is likely to be in surplus or deficit, rather than simply listing where sand comes from."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Interconnected processes forming coastal landforms","t":"Weathering, mass movement, and wave, fluvial and aeolian erosion break down and remove material; transportation (including longshore drift) then moves it before deposition builds new forms. These flows of energy and material create the classic erosional sequence - headland, cave, geo, blow hole, arch, stack, stump - on resistant, high energy coasts, and depositional forms such as beaches, spits, bars, tombolos and salt marshes on lower energy coasts. A top-level answer explains a landform as the product of a specific process, or sequence of processes, acting on a specific rock type or sediment supply, rather than simply naming it."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Coastal landforms as inter-related characteristic landscapes","t":"Individual landforms do not exist in isolation: on a high energy coastline such as Holderness, retreating till cliffs supply the sediment that longshore drift then builds into features such as Spurn Head; on a low energy coastline such as the Mississippi delta, river-supplied sediment builds distributary channels, natural levees and marshland that shift over centuries as delta lobes switch. Change operates across vastly different timescales, from a cliff collapse in seconds to spit growth over millennia. A top-level answer uses named, located evidence from both required case studies to show how the landforms of a landscape connect into one system, and how and why that system changes over different timescales."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Emergent coastal landscapes as sea level falls","t":"Where a cooling climate has locked water into ice sheets, or where land has rebounded after ice unloading, relative sea level falls and former wave-cut platforms are stranded above the reach of the sea as raised beaches and marine terraces, with the former cliff line left as an abandoned (relict) cliff. These landforms are then reshaped by present-day processes - chiefly subaerial weathering rather than marine erosion - because the sea no longer reaches them directly. A top-level answer clearly distinguishes the past process that created the landform (falling sea level) from the different, present-day process now modifying it."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Submergent coastal landscapes as sea level rises","t":"Where a warming climate melts ice sheets and causes thermal expansion of the ocean, relative sea level rises and drowns existing landscapes: river valleys become rias, glacially over-deepened valleys become fjords, and low-lying coastal plains can be reworked into shingle beaches as sediment is pushed landward. As with emergent landscapes, present and future climate and sea level change continue to modify these inherited landforms. A top-level answer names the specific landform produced (ria, fjord or shingle beach) and explains both its original submergence and how it is being altered today."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Intentional human change: managing a coastal landscape","t":"Where a coastline is managed - for example by building groynes or carrying out off-shore dredging - the strategy is chosen deliberately to alter flows of energy and material through the system, typically to trap or add sediment and reduce wave energy reaching the shore. This intentional change to the sediment budget then alters coastal landforms, such as beach profile, which in turn changes the wider landscape, for example by extending the shoreline seawards where sediment accumulates. A top-level answer follows this whole chain - strategy, intended impact on flows, resulting landform change, landscape-scale consequence - using a named, located management scheme, rather than describing the scheme alone."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Unintentional human change: economic development","t":"Economic development such as port construction, trade-route dredging or tourist resort building is not undertaken to change the coast, but it can still disturb flows of material and energy through the system, for example by disturbing the balance of a sediment cell. The resulting effect on landforms (such as an altered beach profile) then has a consequence for the landscape, such as coastal retreat where sediment supply is reduced. A top-level answer treats the human activity as an unintended input into the system, and traces its knock-on effect through processes, landforms and landscape exactly as it would for an intentional scheme."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sediment budget calculation for a stretch of coastline","steps":["Inputs for the cell in one year: 12,000 m3 from cliff erosion + 3,000 m3 from river sediment = 15,000 m3.","Outputs for the same year: 9,000 m3 removed by longshore drift out of the cell + 4,000 m3 lost offshore = 13,000 m3.","Net budget = inputs minus outputs = 15,000 - 13,000 = +2,000 m3, so the beach in this cell is gaining sediment overall."]},{"title":"Calculating a rate of erosion from map evidence","steps":["Cliff-top position measured 38 m further inland on the 2020 map than on the 1990 map.","Time elapsed = 2020 - 1990 = 30 years.","Rate of erosion = 38 divided by 30 = 1.27 m per year, to two decimal places."]},{"title":"Explaining why Mappleton was protected but Great Cowden was not","steps":["Mappleton's coast road and a small number of homes sat directly on an actively retreating cliff line, so the council judged the properties worth defending.","A rock revetment and two rock groynes were built in 1991-92 to absorb wave energy and hold beach sediment in front of the village.","By trapping drift moving south, the scheme starved beaches beyond Mappleton, so erosion at Great Cowden has been reported to accelerate rather than slow."]},{"title":"Converting an erosion rate for use in a written answer","steps":["A quoted retreat rate of 1.8 m per year, if it stayed constant for a century, gives 1.8 x 100 = 180 m of retreat.","Converting to centimetres: 1.8 m/yr x 100 = 180 cm/yr, useful for comparing against seasonal beach-profile change measured in cm.","Flagging the assumption: real retreat is episodic (sudden cliff falls), so a straight-line multiplication likely understates short bursts and overstates quiet years."]},{"title":"Building a Level 4 point on emergent landscapes","steps":["Point: raised beaches on an emergent coastline are relict features rather than a sign the coast is still falling today.","Evidence: a raised beach standing several metres above the current high-tide line records a sea level that was once higher relative to the land, not a present-day process.","Reasoning: because present-day waves rarely reach the old platform, subaerial weathering rather than marine erosion now dominates its modification, linking the landform to a past rather than a current process regime."]},{"title":"Weighing a 'to what extent' judgement","steps":["The case for physical control: Holderness retreats fastest where till cliffs face the longest North Sea fetch, with no management at all along many undefended sections.","The case for human control: at Mappleton, erosion nearly stopped where rock defences were installed, while it accelerated immediately downdrift at Great Cowden.","Judgement: because the same geology produces very different outcomes depending on whether defences are present, human intervention is shown to be the decisive short-term control, even though geology sets the underlying vulnerability."]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the input-process-output structure of a coastal system called?","a":"A systems approach (or systems framework)."},{"q":"Which kind of landform would you expect to dominate a low energy, low relief coastline rather than a high energy rocky one?","a":"Depositional landforms such as beaches, spits or salt marshes, rather than erosional landforms such as cliffs and stacks."},{"q":"If sediment outputs from a cell exceed inputs, what happens to the coastline within that cell?","a":"It is in deficit and the coastline is likely to erode or retreat."},{"q":"Which changes sea level over millennia, and which changes a beach profile in seconds?","a":"Climate-driven sea level change (emergence or submergence) operates over millennia; storm wave impact reshaping a beach profile can happen in seconds to hours."}]},"room":{"facts":["Rate of erosion = total retreat distance divided by the number of years elapsed (e.g. m/yr); multiply or divide to convert between m, cm and km.","The coastline of England and Wales is divided into 11 major sediment cells, each acting as a largely self-contained budget of sediment inputs, transfers and outputs.","Longshore drift moves sediment along a beach in the direction set by the dominant angle of wave approach: swash pushes material up the beach at that angle, backwash pulls it straight back down under gravity.","A sediment budget balances inputs (fluvial, cliff erosion, offshore, aeolian, human nourishment) against outputs (longshore drift out of the cell, offshore transport, aeolian loss); if outputs exceed inputs, the cell is in deficit and the coastline tends to erode.","Erosional landform sequence on a headland: crack/joint, exploited by hydraulic action and abrasion, widens into a cave, may break through to form a geo or blow hole, enlarges into an arch, whose collapsing roof leaves a stack, which erodes further to a stump."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two outputs of a coastal landscape system.","a":"Any two valid outputs, e.g. sediment leaving the sediment cell (via longshore drift or offshore transport) and wave/tidal energy dissipated on the shore.","sol":"Two valid outputs of a coastal system are: (1) sediment removed from the cell by longshore drift into a neighbouring cell, and (2) wave energy that is dissipated as waves break and run up the beach, having been transformed from open-water wave energy into swash and backwash.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a system releases once material and energy have moved through it, not what enters it.","Match each output to the opposite of an input you already know for this system.","Two short, separately labelled points are enough at this tariff, with no explanation required."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify two landforms that are predominantly formed by erosion on a high energy coastline.","a":"Any two from: bays, headlands, cliffs, shore platforms, geos, blow holes, caves, arches, stacks, stumps.","sol":"Two erosional landforms are a headland, formed where a band of more resistant rock protrudes as weaker rock either side is cut back, and a wave-cut (shore) platform, the gently sloping rock surface left behind as a cliff retreats under repeated wave attack.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the sequence of shapes cut into resistant rock as waves attack a coastline.","Choose from the specification's own list of erosional forms rather than inventing new terms.","Name each landform in a single word or short phrase; no process explanation is needed here."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between swash and backwash.","a":"Swash is the forward movement of water and sediment up the beach as a wave breaks, driven by the wave's forward momentum; backwash is the return flow of that water back down the beach under gravity. Which one dominates determines whether a beach gains or loses sediment.","sol":"Swash is the surge of water, and any sediment it carries, moving up the beach face as a wave breaks, driven by the wave's forward momentum. Backwash is the water draining back down the beach face afterwards, pulled by gravity rather than by any forward wave force. The balance between the two matters because it controls net sediment movement: on a constructive wave, swash is stronger than backwash, so more material is carried up the beach than is dragged back down, and the beach builds up; on a destructive wave, backwash is stronger than swash, so net sediment is pulled back down the beach, and the beach is eroded.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the two halves of a single wave's journey up and back down the beach.","Consider which direction each half moves in, what causes that movement, and why the balance between them matters for the beach.","Explain both halves, with a reason for the direction of movement, rather than giving a single bare definition of each."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain how geology influences the formation of erosional landforms on a rocky coastline.","a":"Lithology (rock strength/resistance) controls the rate of erosion; structure (dip, jointing, alternating resistant/weak bands) controls where and in what shapes erosion is concentrated, e.g. discordant coastlines producing headlands and bays.","sol":"Lithology determines how quickly a rock erodes: a resistant rock such as granite withstands hydraulic action and abrasion far longer than a weak, unconsolidated rock such as boulder clay, which can retreat metres in a single storm. Structure then determines where that erosion is concentrated: where bands of resistant and weaker rock lie at right angles to the coast (a discordant coastline), the weaker rock is cut back into bays while the resistant rock survives as headlands; where joints or faults create lines of weakness, waves exploit these to open geos, and to cut caves that may progress to arches, stacks and stumps as the roof collapses. Geology therefore explains both the rate and the exact location and shape of erosional landforms.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the rock properties waves are working against, beyond simply 'hard' or 'soft'.","Remember that 'explain' means giving the reasons behind erosion patterns, not just naming or describing landforms you would find on a rocky coastline.","Structure your answer in two linked parts, each explaining a different aspect of the geology."],"codes":["1.b","2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how the construction of a harbour wall, built to shelter a port rather than to manage erosion, can unintentionally disrupt the sediment budget of a coastal landscape system.","a":"The wall blocks the longshore movement of sediment, so sand accumulates on the updrift side of the structure while the downdrift beach stops receiving new sediment, pushing that stretch's budget into deficit and causing it to erode, even though the wall was not built with the coast in mind.","sol":"A harbour wall is built to shelter a port entrance from waves, not to manage the coastline, but because it projects out into the surf zone it interrupts the longshore drift carrying sediment along the beach. On the updrift side of the wall, sediment that would otherwise have continued past is trapped and builds up, widening the beach there. On the downdrift side, the beach can no longer be resupplied by drift from updrift, so its sediment budget swings into deficit as wave action keeps removing material with nothing arriving to replace it, and the coastline there narrows and retreats. This mirrors the effect of a deliberately built groyne, but here the interruption to the sediment budget is an unintended by-product of a structure built for an entirely different, non-coastal purpose.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about a structure built to shelter shipping, not to manage the coast, that still sticks out into the surf zone.","Consider what happens to sediment travelling along the beach when it meets a solid barrier it cannot pass.","Explain the effect on one side of the structure, then the different effect on the other side, before linking both to a budget change."],"codes":["1.c","4.b"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain how a coastal landscape has been managed to reduce erosion.","a":"Named UK scheme, e.g. Mappleton, Holderness: rock groynes/revetment built to trap sediment and absorb wave energy, protecting the village but altering longshore drift and sediment supply further along the coast.","sol":"At Mappleton on the Holderness coast, two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built in 1991-92 to protect the village and the coast road from an eroding till cliff. The groynes trap sediment moving south by longshore drift, building up a wider protective beach in front of Mappleton, while the revetment absorbs and reflects wave energy before it reaches the cliff foot. This intentional interruption of the drift has starved beaches further south, such as at Great Cowden, of sediment they previously received, so erosion there has been reported to accelerate even as Mappleton itself has been stabilised.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall a UK case where a specific structure, not just a policy name, was built to hold a village's coastline.","Explain what the structure physically does to wave energy or to sediment moving along the shore.","End by describing the effect the scheme had beyond the immediate site it was built to protect."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"A cliff top was measured 54 m further inland in 2005 than it had been in 1975. Calculate the average annual rate of erosion in cm per year, and comment on what this rate suggests about the coastline.","a":"54 m divided by 30 years = 1.8 m/yr = 180 cm/yr; a rate this high suggests a soft, easily eroded coastline (e.g. till) rather than a resistant rock coast.","sol":"Time elapsed = 2005 - 1975 = 30 years. Rate of erosion = 54 m divided by 30 years = 1.8 m per year. Converting to centimetres: 1.8 m/yr x 100 = 180 cm per year. A retreat rate of this size is high for a UK coastline, close to the coast's long-term average for Holderness, with hotspots nearer 4 m per year, and points to a weak, unconsolidated lithology such as glacial till being attacked by a long-fetch, high-energy wave climate, rather than a resistant rock coastline, which would typically retreat at a small fraction of this rate.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out how much time separates the two survey dates before doing anything else.","Find the rate in metres per year first, then convert to the unit the question actually asks for.","Give your numerical answer, then relate the size of that number to the kind of coastline that would produce it."],"codes":["2.a","2.b"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain how economic development has unintentionally changed a coastal landscape.","a":"Named beyond-UK example, e.g. Nile delta and the Aswan High Dam: development for irrigation/power reduces sediment supply, altering landforms and causing coastal retreat.","sol":"On the Nile delta, large-scale irrigation and hydroelectric development were the goals of the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, not coastal change; but by trapping the great majority of the Nile's sediment load in Lake Nasser, the dam removed the sediment that had built and maintained the delta coast for millennia. Longshore currents continue to erode the Rosetta and Damietta promontories, but with far less new sediment arriving, the beach profile along these promontories has steepened and narrowed, and the shoreline has retreated, threatening farmland, fish farms and low-lying settlement close to the coast.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Choose a beyond-UK example where the development's original purpose had nothing to do with the coastline itself.","Identify the specific flow of material the development interrupted before describing the coastal effect.","Trace your answer through from the development, to the landform change, to the wider landscape consequence."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"A beach profile survey recorded a beach width of 42 m in summer and 27 m in winter. Calculate the percentage decrease in beach width between the two surveys.","a":"Decrease = 15 m; 15 divided by 42, times 100, is approximately 35.7%.","sol":"Decrease in width = 42 m - 27 m = 15 m. Percentage decrease = (15 divided by 42) x 100 = approximately 35.7%. This roughly one-third narrowing is typical of the seasonal switch from gentler, constructive summer waves to steeper, destructive winter waves, which pull sediment offshore into a bar rather than leaving it on the visible beach.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the actual change in width in metres before turning it into a percentage.","Divide that change by the original (summer) width, then convert to a percentage.","Give the percentage to one decimal place with the working that produced it shown."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"To what extent do physical factors alone explain the differences between high energy and low energy coastlines?","a":"Should weigh wave energy/fetch and geology against human influences (management, altered sediment supply), using named high- and low-energy case studies, reaching a qualified judgement.","sol":"Physical factors go a long way towards explaining the contrast between high energy and low energy coastlines. Wave energy, driven by fetch, wind speed and duration, is the most direct physical control: Holderness faces a long North Sea fetch generating powerful, destructive waves that attack weak glacial till, producing a rapidly retreating, erosional, high energy landscape of crumbling till cliffs. By contrast, the Mississippi delta sits in the low wave energy, microtidal Gulf of Mexico, where river-supplied sediment can accumulate faster than the weak waves remove it, producing a low energy, depositional landscape of distributary channels, levees and marsh. Geology reinforces this physical contrast, since Holderness's unconsolidated till could not withstand even a moderate wave climate, whereas a resistant rock coastline exposed to the same North Sea fetch would retreat far more slowly, showing that lithology and wave energy interact rather than acting alone. However, physical factors are not the whole story. At Holderness, human management at Mappleton, where rock groynes and a revetment were built in 1991-92, has locally reduced erosion to a fraction of the unmanaged rate despite the wave climate and geology being unchanged, while accelerating erosion at undefended Great Cowden further along the same coast; the difference between these two sites cannot be explained by physical factors at all. At the Mississippi delta, the logic runs in reverse: extensive levee building for flood control and navigation, and upstream damming, has cut the sediment supply that the delta's naturally low wave energy alone would not have removed, so wetland and land loss have accelerated regardless of the wave climate remaining calm. On balance, physical factors, principally wave energy/fetch and geology, set the fundamental potential for a coastline to be high or low energy, and explain most of the contrast between an undefended, high energy Holderness and a naturally low energy Mississippi delta. But the case studies show that human intervention, intentional at Mappleton and unintentional at the Mississippi delta, can measurably override that physical potential at specific, well-documented sites, so physical factors alone do not fully explain outcomes everywhere.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Consider both the case for physical control and for human control across your two case studies.","Use your high energy and low energy examples as the direct evidence for each side of the argument.","Build towards a conclusion that states how far you agree, rather than describing each factor in turn."],"codes":["1.b","2.b"]},{"q":"'Human intervention has a greater impact on coastal landscape systems than physical processes.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"16-mark essay: define terms, develop paragraphs contrasting physical process evidence with human intervention evidence, intentional and unintentional, from named case studies, evaluate throughout, reach a qualified judgement.","sol":"Physical processes remain the foundation of every coastal system: wave energy generated by wind and fetch, and the lithology and structure of the coast, set the underlying rate and style of change, as shown by the rapid natural retreat of the weak till cliffs of Holderness under a powerful North Sea wave climate. Human intervention, however, can locally overwhelm this physical control. At Mappleton, the rock groynes and revetment built in 1991-92 intentionally interrupted longshore drift and wave energy, reducing local erosion to a small fraction of the unmanaged rate, while accelerating erosion further along the coast at Great Cowden, a change in the sediment budget with no equivalent shift in the physical wave or geological regime. Unintentional intervention can be equally decisive: the Aswan High Dam was never built to affect the coast, yet by trapping the Nile's sediment load it converted a naturally maintained delta coastline into a retreating one, a reversal that physical processes alone cannot explain. Taken together, physical processes explain the underlying potential and pace of change a coastline would show left alone, but the case studies show that human intervention, whether intentional or not, can now match or exceed that physical control at specific, well-documented sites, so the statement holds to a considerable extent, though not everywhere, since many undefended stretches of coast still change overwhelmingly under physical control alone.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set the physical baseline before bringing in the human cases that complicate it.","Draw on both an intentional and an unintentional example of human impact for your strongest answer.","Reach a conclusion that states how far you agree, acknowledging where physical control still dominates."],"codes":["1.b","4.a","4.b"]},{"q":"How far do the landforms of an emergent coastline reflect present-day processes rather than past sea level change?","a":"Explains raised beaches, marine terraces and abandoned cliffs as products of past sea level fall, then evaluates the extent to which present weathering/modification changes them, reaching a qualified judgement that origin is past and modification is present.","sol":"The defining landforms of an emergent coastline, raised beaches, marine terraces and abandoned cliffs, owe their existence overwhelmingly to a past episode of sea level fall relative to the land, during a previous cooling climate, when a wave-cut platform and its beach deposits were left stranded above the sea's current reach. In this sense, present-day marine processes cannot be said to explain these landforms at all, since the sea no longer reaches them directly to erode or nourish them. However, once formed, these relict landforms are not frozen: present and future climate and sea level change continue to modify them, chiefly through subaerial weathering, mass movement and soil development acting on the abandoned cliff face and the surface of the raised beach, and, where sea level rise now brings the sea back towards a low raised beach, renewed marine reworking is possible. Overall, present-day processes explain the ongoing modification and slow degradation of emergent landforms, but not their origin or their fundamental location and form, which are inherited entirely from a past, different sea level; the landforms are therefore relict features being gradually altered by the present, not features created by it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the process that first made each landform from any process acting on it today.","Consider what type of process, marine or subaerial, can now reach a landform that used to be at sea level.","Conclude with a clear statement of how far 'present-day' can explain what you see, not just yes or no."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify two landforms associated with a submergent coastline.","a":"Any two from: rias, fjords, shingle beaches.","sol":"Two submergent landforms are a ria, a drowned river valley flooded by rising sea level, and a fjord, a drowned, glacially over-deepened valley flooded in the same way but typically steeper-sided with a shallower rock lip near its mouth.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think of landforms created when the sea rises to flood an existing valley shape.","There is more than one shape of valley that can be drowned by rising sea level - make sure your two answers are genuinely different landforms, not two names for the same one.","Name each landform in a word or short phrase; no explanation is required at this tariff."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how sea level change has influenced the landforms of an emergent coastline.","a":"Falling sea level (from a cooling climate or isostatic rebound) strands a former wave-cut platform and beach above current sea level as a raised beach/marine terrace, and the former cliff becomes an abandoned cliff.","sol":"During a previous cooler climatic period, falling sea level, whether from more of the world's water being locked into growing ice sheets or from the land rebounding as ice sheets melted from it, left the wave-cut platform and beach deposits that had formed at the old shoreline stranded above the reach of the present-day sea. These stranded surfaces are preserved as raised beaches and marine terraces, while the cliff that once backed the old shoreline now stands inland of the modern coast as an abandoned cliff, no longer attacked by waves. Sea level change is therefore the direct cause of both the position and the form of these landforms, even though the geomorphic processes that originally cut them, marine erosion, were identical to those shaping an active coastline today.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what causes relative sea level to fall in the first place.","Consider what happens to a shoreline's landforms once the sea that shaped them retreats from them.","Name the resulting landform types as you explain the mechanism that produced them."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify two ways in which wind direction and frequency, and global ocean currents, act as inputs into a coastal landscape system.","a":"Wind direction and frequency determine the dominant angle of wave approach, which sets the direction of longshore drift; ocean currents redistribute heat around the globe and can influence the frequency or intensity of storms reaching a coast, adding to its wave energy input.","sol":"Wind is not just a source of wave energy through its speed, duration and fetch: the direction it blows from, and how frequently it blows from that direction, sets the dominant angle at which waves approach a coastline, which in turn fixes the direction of longshore drift along that shore. Ocean currents are a second, larger-scale input: by redistributing heat around the globe, they influence the atmospheric and sea-surface conditions that generate storms, so a coast lying in the path of a current-linked storm track receives more frequent or more energetic wave inputs than one that does not. Both are therefore inputs into the system in the same sense as fetch or tidal range: external energy and directional information entering the coast from beyond the shore itself.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about wind as more than just a source of wave height - consider what else about the wind matters to a coastline.","Consider a second physical input operating at a much larger, ocean-basin scale than local wind and waves.","Give one point about wind and one point about currents, in a sentence or two each; no full explanation is required at this tariff."],"codes":["1.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is a sediment cell?","a":"A length of coastline, usually bounded by prominent headlands, within which inputs, transfers and outputs of sediment form a largely self-contained system."},{"q":"Name the process that moves sediment along a beach in the direction of the dominant wave approach.","a":"Longshore drift."},{"q":"Give one landform typical of a submergent coastline.","a":"A ria, a fjord, or a shingle beach (any one)."},{"q":"What do you need to know, besides the distance a cliff has retreated, to calculate a rate of erosion?","a":"The length of time, in years, over which that retreat occurred."}]},"cards":[{"front":"What three elements make up a coastal system?","back":"Inputs (e.g. wave and wind energy, sediment), processes (erosion, transport, deposition) and outputs (e.g. sediment leaving the cell, energy dissipated on the shore).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Constructive vs destructive wave - what's the key difference?","back":"Constructive waves have a stronger swash than backwash, building the beach up; destructive waves have a stronger backwash than swash, eroding the beach.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Name the three broad sources of coastal sediment.","back":"Terrestrial (rivers, weathering, mass movement, longshore drift), offshore (marine deposition), and human (beach nourishment).","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Name three erosional processes that shape coastal landforms.","back":"Hydraulic action, abrasion and attrition (solution is a fourth).","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Put in order: cave, arch, stack, stump.","back":"Cave, then arch, then stack, then stump - each stage forms as continued erosion collapses the landform before it.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Which UK coastline is the high-energy case study here?","back":"The Holderness coast, East Yorkshire - soft glacial till retreating rapidly under a powerful North Sea wave climate.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Which beyond-UK coastline is the low-energy case study here?","back":"The Mississippi delta, Louisiana, USA - a low wave-energy, river-dominated depositional coastline.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"What is a raised beach?","back":"A former wave-cut platform and beach stranded above current sea level after relative sea level fell, now modified mainly by weathering rather than waves.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"What is a ria?","back":"A drowned river valley, flooded by rising sea level, widening gradually towards the sea.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"What is a fjord?","back":"A drowned, glacially over-deepened valley flooded by rising sea level - steep-sided, often with a shallower rock lip near its mouth.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"What management was used at Mappleton, Holderness?","back":"Rock groynes and a rock revetment, built in 1991-92, to protect the village and coast road from erosion.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Why did the Aswan High Dam unintentionally damage the Nile delta coast?","back":"It trapped most of the Nile's sediment in Lake Nasser, starving the delta coast of replenishment, so waves now erode faster than the coast rebuilds.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"How do you calculate a rate of coastal erosion?","back":"Divide the total distance the coastline has retreated by the number of years over which that retreat happened.","code":"2.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4","items":["Human intervention has been the decisive control on the Holderness coast wherever it has been applied, even though the underlying geology remains constant along the whole coastline.","At Mappleton, two rock groynes and a rock revetment built in 1991-92 trap longshore drift and absorb wave energy, so the village and its coast road have been stabilised despite standing on the same weak glacial till that retreats rapidly everywhere else along Holderness.","This works because the scheme directly interrupts the sediment budget: by holding sediment that would otherwise continue south, it locally reverses the deficit that drives erosion, while starving beaches further along the coast, such as Great Cowden, of the sediment they previously received, and so accelerating erosion there instead.","The contrast between Mappleton's stability and Cowden's faster retreat, both cut from identical till under an identical wave climate, shows that human management, not geology or wave energy, is the factor separating the two outcomes.","To a considerable extent, then, human intervention now controls how the Holderness coast evolves at any specific, defended site, even though geology and wave energy still set the pace of change everywhere intervention has not reached."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2","items":["Human intervention has affected the Holderness coast a lot.","For example, at Mappleton they built some rock defences to stop erosion, which helped protect the village.","This shows that management can change how much a coastline erodes, because putting in defences means the waves cannot reach the cliff as easily.","So human intervention is quite important for the coast."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Holderness coast (high energy)","items":["Location: East Yorkshire coast, England, running roughly 61 km from Flamborough Head south to Spurn Head.","Geology: soft, unconsolidated glacial till (boulder clay), highly vulnerable to both marine erosion and rainfall-triggered mass movement.","Erosion rate: among the fastest-eroding coastlines in Europe, with an average long-term retreat commonly cited at approximately 1.5-2 m per year, though individual storms can remove several metres in a single event.","Sediment fate: eroded till supplies sand and shingle that longshore drift carries south, feeding the growth of Spurn Head spit at the southern end of the coast.","Historic loss: numerous villages recorded in medieval sources have been lost to the sea along this coast as the cliff line has retreated over the centuries."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Mississippi delta (low energy)","items":["Location: mouth of the Mississippi River, Louisiana, south-eastern USA, extending into the low wave-energy, microtidal Gulf of Mexico.","Landform type: a bird's-foot (digitate) delta, built by fluvial deposition through branching distributary channels bordered by natural levees.","Timescale of change: the delta has built a series of distinct lobes over approximately the last 7,000 years as the river has periodically switched course, abandoning older lobes to subside and erode.","Coastal wetland loss: coastal Louisiana's marshes and wetlands have shrunk substantially over the twentieth century, a change widely attributed to reduced sediment and freshwater reaching the delta plain.","Energy setting: low wave energy and a small tidal range allow river-supplied sediment to accumulate faster than it is removed, producing the delta's depositional, low-relief landforms."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Mappleton coastal management, Holderness","items":["Location: the village of Mappleton, on the actively eroding Holderness coast, East Yorkshire.","Strategy: two rock groynes and a rock revetment, constructed in 1991-92 by the local council.","Reason: to protect the village, the B1242 coast road and surrounding farmland from an eroding till cliff that threatened to cut the road within a few years.","Intentional impact: the groynes trap sediment moving south by longshore drift, widening the beach and reducing the wave energy reaching the cliff foot in front of the village.","Consequence beyond the site: with less sediment passing south of Mappleton, erosion has been reported to accelerate along the undefended coast further south, such as at Great Cowden, illustrating how defending one point on a coastline can increase erosion downdrift."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Nile delta economic development, Egypt","items":["Location: the Nile delta, northern Egypt, where the Nile splits into the Rosetta and Damietta distributaries before reaching the Mediterranean Sea.","Economic development: intensive agriculture (notably cotton and rice) on the delta's fertile silts, extensive fishing and aquaculture (including Lake Manzala and Lake Burullus), and major ports such as Alexandria, Damietta and Port Said.","Key intervention: the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, built for irrigation control and hydroelectric power rather than to change the coast.","Unintentional impact: the dam traps the great majority of the Nile's sediment load in the reservoir (Lake Nasser) behind it, sharply reducing the sediment reaching the delta coast.","Consequence: with far less sediment to replace what waves remove, the Rosetta and Damietta promontories have retreated significantly in the decades since the dam was completed, threatening farmland, fish farms and low-lying settlement close to the coast."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'The evolution of coastal landscapes owes more to human activity than to physical processes.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (16 marks)","items":["Decoded question: weigh physical controls (wave energy, geology, sea level history) against human controls (intentional management and unintentional development) on landform and landscape change, and reach a stated, qualified judgement on which matters more.","Paragraph 1 - establish the physical baseline: use wave energy/fetch and geology to explain the underlying difference between a high energy coastline (Holderness) and a low energy coastline (Mississippi delta), showing what physical processes alone would produce.","Paragraph 2 - intentional human change: use the Mappleton rock groynes and revetment to show management directly overriding the physical baseline at a specific, defended site, while shifting the problem to an undefended site nearby.","Paragraph 3 - unintentional human change: use the Aswan High Dam's effect on the Nile delta's sediment supply to show that even development with no coastal purpose can reverse a coastline's natural behaviour.","Evaluation thread: in each paragraph, weigh the human factor against the physical baseline it is acting on, rather than treating the two as separate lists - keep returning to whether the human change could have happened without the physical setting (weak till, sediment-starved delta) that made it effective.","Conclusion must state: a qualified judgement that human activity now exerts the stronger control at specific, well-documented sites, but that physical processes still set the underlying potential and dominate wherever no intervention has occurred, so the statement is true to a considerable but not total extent."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T06:57:50.978Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Content, case studies and answer keys are sound - the fact files are all coastal, the required set is complete and correctly balanced (Holderness UK high-energy, Mississippi delta beyond-UK low-energy, Mappleton UK managed, Nile delta beyond-UK used-by-people, giving two beyond-UK plus UK), all nine key ideas are covered in the board's terms and each lesson section closes with what a top-level answer does, both calculations re-solve exactly (54/30 = 1.8 m/yr = 180 cm/yr; 15/42 = 35.7%) as do all four worked examples, and the Level 4 / Level 2 model paragraphs are genuinely and visibly different; minor polish only beyond the listed problems - questions[2] uses 'State', which is not among the board's command words ('Explain the difference' or 'Define' would fit), questions[6].sol calls 1.8 m/yr 'comparable to the fastest-eroding sections of the Holderness coast' when that is nearer its long-term average than its 4 m/yr hotspots, the two calculation questions are coded 2.a/2.b rather than to the topic-skills strand, and the specification's wind 'direction and frequency' and the global pattern of ocean currents get one thin sentence in lesson idea 1.b and no question at all."}}},"1.2":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Topic 1.2 forms Section B of the Physical systems paper (01) — Section A covers Landscape Systems, and Section B is Earth's Life Support Systems: examiners require a precise, systems-based account of how water and carbon move between the land, oceans, atmosphere and cryosphere, backed by two compulsory named case studies — a tropical rainforest and the Arctic tundra — and by the simple mass balance, rate-of-flow and unit-conversion skills tested from the Resource Booklet. Marks are lost when a student describes the cycles vaguely instead of naming specific stores, flows and processes, and when a case study is asserted rather than used with real places, dates and figures to answer the question set. AO2 (application) carries the largest share of marks across the qualification, so this room drills using the case studies and the water/carbon budget ideas to answer ‘to what extent’ and ‘evaluate’ style questions, not just reciting content.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Why water and carbon matter, and how they move","t":"Water sustains every organism: humans need it to drink, grow food and generate power; flora depends on it for photosynthesis and turgor; fauna depends on it directly and through the habitats it creates. Carbon is the building block of all life on Earth — it forms the organic molecules in every living cell — and is available for use both in the natural world (through photosynthesis and food chains) and by humans (through fossil fuels and farming). Both elements cycle continuously between the land, oceans and atmosphere: the global water cycle is best understood as a closed system (the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant, even as it changes state and location) while individual drainage basins and forests behave as open systems, gaining and losing water and carbon across their boundaries. A top-level answer states explicitly which scale — global or local — it is describing as open or closed, rather than treating the two cycles as vaguely ‘linked’ without naming the store, flow or boundary involved."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Stores, inputs and outputs of the two systems","t":"The specification names seven major stores shared or split between the two cycles: the atmosphere, oceans, water bodies, ice (the cryosphere), soil, vegetation and groundwater — and these vary hugely in size, from the ocean (the largest water store) to the much smaller store held in living vegetation. The water cycle's main inputs and outputs are precipitation bringing water into a store, and evapotranspiration removing it; snowmelt (ablation) is different again — it is an output from a snow or ice store, but at the same time an input to a catchment's liquid water store, since the meltwater it releases feeds rivers, soil moisture and groundwater. The carbon cycle's inputs and outputs include precipitation, photosynthesis and chemical weathering drawing carbon down, and decomposition, respiration and combustion releasing it back. A top-level answer names the specific store being discussed (not just ‘the ground’) and states whether a named process is acting as an input or an output for that store, since examiners reward this precision over a general description of ‘the cycle’."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Processes and pathways within the water and carbon cycles","t":"Water moves through a landscape via evaporation, transpiration, condensation (forming clouds), precipitation, interception (by the vegetation canopy), ablation, and runoff — including overland flow and saturated overland flow — before reaching a channel; catchment hydrology further breaks this down into infiltration, percolation, throughflow, groundwater flow and, in cold environments, cryospheric processes such as accumulation, ablation (meltwater generation) and sublimation, together with permafrost limiting infiltration to a shallow active layer. Carbon moves through photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition and combustion (both natural, such as wildfire, and human, such as burning fossil fuels), with natural sequestration locking carbon away in oceans, vegetation, sediments and through the weathering of rock. A top-level answer traces a specific pathway step by step — for example from interception through throughflow to a river channel — rather than naming the processes as an unconnected list, showing the sequence an examiner is looking for."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Case study: the tropical rainforest (Amazon basin)","t":"The specification requires a named case study of a tropical rainforest covering the water and carbon cycles specific to that biome, from an individual tree up to the rainforest as a whole. High, constant temperature and heavy rainfall drive rapid evapotranspiration, photosynthesis and decomposition, while porous, easily weathered rock and dense vegetation cover create distinct water and carbon stores in the canopy, litter layer and soil. For one drainage basin, deforestation and farming have changed these flows and stores — removing interception and transpiration, increasing overland flow and exposing soil carbon to faster decomposition — while strategies such as afforestation and improved agricultural techniques (for example agroforestry) can restore some of these positive effects. A top-level answer uses this case study's own places, figures and dates to answer the specific question set, rather than describing rainforests in general terms."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Case study: the Arctic tundra","t":"The specification also requires a named case study of the Arctic tundra covering its own distinct water and carbon cycles, rates of flow and stores. Low temperatures slow decomposition, so organic matter builds up in the soil rather than being broken down, while permafrost — ground frozen for at least two consecutive years — restricts infiltration and groundwater flow, confining the active layer of seasonal thaw to a shallow surface zone. Seasonal change is extreme: a short summer thaw releases stored water and carbon that refreeze again in winter. The developing oil and gas industry, such as at Alaska's Prudhoe Bay, disturbs both cycles through extraction and infrastructure, and is moderated by management strategies including elevated pipelines and protected land. A top-level answer explains how the case study's cold, seasonal conditions specifically shape its cycles, rather than transferring rainforest explanations to a different biome."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Dynamic equilibrium and human disturbance of the cycles","t":"Dynamic equilibrium describes a system in long-term balance, where inputs and outputs stay roughly equal so stores remain broadly stable despite constant short-term change. Human activity disturbs this balance: land use change (urban growth, farming, forestry) alters how much water infiltrates or runs off, and how much carbon is stored in vegetation and soil; water extraction (surface and groundwater, including from aquifers and artesian basins) directly reduces stores; and fossil fuel combustion, offset only partly by carbon sequestration, adds carbon to the atmosphere faster than natural processes remove it. These disturbances can trigger feedback loops — positive feedback amplifies the original change (for example permafrost thaw releasing methane that drives further warming and thaw), while negative feedback dampens it. A top-level answer identifies whether a named human factor pushes a system away from or back towards equilibrium, and whether any feedback loop that follows is positive or negative."},{"code":"3.b","h":"How the cycles change over time","t":"Change operates across very different timescales. Short-term changes — diurnal and seasonal shifts in climate, temperature, sunlight and foliage — alter rates of photosynthesis, evapotranspiration and river discharge within a single day or year, and matter because they can reverse quickly. Long-term changes, unfolding over millions of years, alter the stores and flows themselves, for example through the slow burial of carbon in sediment or the growth and retreat of ice sheets. Research and monitoring techniques (such as satellite data, ice cores and river gauging) are essential to identify and record these changes at both timescales, because the significance of a change cannot be judged without a long enough record to compare it against. A top-level answer states the timescale a change operates over before assessing its significance, rather than treating all change as equally rapid or equally permanent."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Linking the two cycles","t":"The water and carbon cycles are not separate systems: they link and depend on each other through the oceans (which absorb and release both CO2 and water vapour), the atmosphere (which transports both), the cryosphere (which locks up both frozen water and frozen carbon in permafrost) and vegetation (which draws down carbon while transpiring water). Human activities change the availability of both fossil and terrestrial carbon and water stores by using them as resources — extracting groundwater and burning fossil fuels alike remove a store faster than it is naturally replenished. Long-term climate change, driven mainly by disturbance to the carbon cycle, in turn reshapes the water cycle through altered precipitation patterns and glacial melt. A top-level answer traces a specific linking mechanism (a named store or flow shared by both cycles) rather than asserting that the cycles are ‘connected’ without saying how."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Managing water and carbon at the global scale","t":"Because the two cycles operate at a planetary scale, management strategies are needed at that scale too. Carbon cycle strategies include afforestation and wetland restoration (both restoring natural sequestration), improving agricultural practices, and reducing emissions through mechanisms such as carbon trading and international agreements. Water cycle strategies include improving forestry techniques, allocating water fairly between domestic, industrial and agricultural users, and drainage basin planning that manages run-off, surface stores and groundwater together rather than separately. A top-level answer evaluates a named strategy against a specific criterion — its effectiveness, its cost, or how equitably it is applied — and reaches a judgement on how far global management can realistically balance protection of these cycles against continuing human demand, rather than simply listing strategies."}],"examples":[{"title":"Simple mass balance — a perennial snow patch in the Arctic tundra","steps":["A perennial snow patch — one that does not fully melt away each summer, so its size can be tracked from one year to the next like a small glacier — records winter accumulation of 280 mm water equivalent of snow.","The same patch records summer ablation of 340 mm water equivalent through melt.","Net balance = 280 mm − 340 mm = −60 mm water equivalent for the year.","A negative balance means the patch lost more mass than it gained this year, so it shrank and left less insulating snow in place to protect the permafrost beneath it, allowing the active layer to thaw more deeply."]},{"title":"Calculating river discharge — an Amazon tributary","steps":["The channel is 10 m wide with a mean depth of 2 m.","Cross-sectional area = 10 m × 2 m = 20 m².","Mean velocity is measured at 0.6 m/s, so discharge = 20 m² × 0.6 m/s = 12 cumecs.","12 cumecs converts to 12,000 litres per second, since 1 m³ equals 1,000 litres."]},{"title":"Converting a carbon store figure — rainforest soil","steps":["A hectare of rainforest soil is measured as storing 120 tonnes of carbon.","1 hectare = 10,000 m², so 120 tonnes ÷ 10,000 m² = 0.012 tonnes of carbon per m².","0.012 tonnes = 12 kg, since 1 tonne = 1,000 kg.","So the soil stores approximately 12 kg of carbon per m², a figure that can then be compared against a different biome's soil carbon density."]},{"title":"Applying the water budget equation — a small drainage basin","steps":["Over a year, the basin receives 1,800 mm of precipitation (P).","River discharge at the basin's outlet totals 700 mm (Q) and evapotranspiration losses are estimated at 1,050 mm (ET).","Using P = Q + ET ± ΔS: 1,800 = 700 + 1,050 ± ΔS, so ΔS = 1,800 − 1,750 = +50 mm.","The positive ΔS shows the basin's stores (soil moisture and groundwater) grew by 50 mm over the year rather than staying exactly in balance."]},{"title":"Weighing physical against human causes — Arctic tundra oil and gas","steps":["Permafrost naturally stores carbon frozen in place for thousands of years, released only slowly through the natural summer thaw of the active layer.","Since production began at Alaska's Prudhoe Bay oil field in 1977, infrastructure and the warming linked to fossil fuel combustion have accelerated permafrost thaw in the surrounding area.","This human-driven acceleration adds to, rather than replaces, the natural seasonal thaw cycle already present in the tundra.","A top-level answer keeps both causes in view and states which one is doing more of the work at the timescale the question asks about, rather than treating the change as purely human or purely natural."]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between an open and a closed system in the context of the water and carbon cycles?","a":"A closed system has no matter crossing its boundary, though energy can (e.g. the global water cycle, since the total amount of water on Earth stays broadly constant); an open system has both matter and energy crossing its boundary (e.g. a single drainage basin, which gains water as precipitation and loses it as river discharge and evapotranspiration)."},{"q":"Name one input and one output of the carbon cycle.","a":"Any one input (e.g. photosynthesis or weathering) and one output (e.g. respiration or combustion) — for example photosynthesis (input, converting atmospheric CO2 into stored carbon in plants) and respiration (output, releasing CO2 back to the atmosphere)."},{"q":"Give one physical factor that affects flows and stores in the water cycle.","a":"Any one of temperature, rock permeability and porosity, or relief (e.g. steep relief speeds up overland flow and reduces the time available for infiltration)."},{"q":"What is dynamic equilibrium in the water and carbon cycles?","a":"A state of long-term balance in a system, where inputs and outputs are roughly equal so that stores stay broadly the same size over time, even though short-term fluctuations occur."}]},"room":{"facts":["Water budget equation: precipitation (P) = river discharge (Q) + evapotranspiration (ET) ± change in storage (ΔS).","Simple mass balance: net balance = accumulation − ablation; a positive balance means a store (such as snow, ice or soil moisture) is gaining mass, a negative balance means it is losing mass.","River discharge (cumecs, m³/s) = cross-sectional area (width × mean depth) × mean velocity; 1 m³ = 1,000 litres.","Approximately 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, but approximately 68% of the planet's freshwater is locked in ice and glaciers.","The Amazon basin covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine South American countries, and its river discharges an average of roughly 209,000 cumecs into the Atlantic Ocean — the largest discharge of any river on Earth.","Arctic permafrost is estimated to store around 1,500 billion tonnes of carbon — roughly twice the amount currently held in the atmosphere."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two forms of chemical weathering that act as an input to the carbon cycle, and briefly explain how one of them draws carbon into a store.","a":"Two of: carbonation, hydrolysis (chemical weathering of rock, drawing carbon into solution or into new minerals) — e.g. carbonation: rainwater carrying dissolved CO2 reacts with rock such as limestone to form soluble compounds, carrying the carbon away in solution.","sol":"Chemical weathering acts as an input to the carbon cycle by breaking down rock and drawing carbon into new stores. Two accepted processes are carbonation, in which rainwater containing dissolved CO2 reacts with rock such as limestone to form soluble compounds, and hydrolysis, in which water reacts chemically with silicate minerals in rock to break them down, releasing ions that carry some carbon away in solution. One mark is given for each of the two correctly named processes, and the third mark is for a brief explanation of how one of them moves carbon into a store, as in either example above.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall which specific term in the specification's list of carbon cycle inputs covers rock breakdown by chemical reaction.","Think of this as weathering that happens through a chemical reaction with rock, not weathering caused by frost, plants or temperature change alone.","Name two separate processes, then add one short sentence on how just one of them moves carbon into a store — that extra sentence is what earns the third mark."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between an open system and a closed system, using an example of each from the water or carbon cycle.","a":"Closed = only energy crosses the boundary (e.g. the global water cycle, total water broadly constant); open = both matter and energy cross the boundary (e.g. a drainage basin, gaining and losing water).","sol":"A closed system is one in which energy can cross the system boundary but matter cannot; the global water cycle is usually treated this way, since the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant even as it changes location and state. An open system is one in which both matter and energy cross the boundary; a single drainage basin is an open system because it gains water and sediment as inputs (precipitation) and loses them as outputs (river discharge, evapotranspiration) that leave the system entirely. Full marks require a correct definition and a correctly matched, named example for each system type: two marks for the closed-system definition and example, two for the open-system definition and example, and two for explaining why each example fits its definition rather than simply asserting it.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is allowed to cross a system's boundary in each case — is it only energy, or matter as well?","Choose one global-scale example and one smaller-scale example from the water or carbon cycle to illustrate the contrast.","Give one definition and one named example for each type of system, in two clearly separated parts of your answer."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain how evapotranspiration links the land surface to the atmosphere in the water cycle.","a":"Evapotranspiration combines evaporation from water/soil surfaces and transpiration from plants, moving water vapour from the land into the atmosphere as an input to future precipitation.","sol":"Evapotranspiration is the combined loss of water vapour from the land surface into the atmosphere through evaporation (from open water, soil and other wet surfaces) and transpiration (water vapour released through the stomata of plants during photosynthesis). Because this water vapour rises into the atmosphere, it becomes available to condense and fall again as precipitation elsewhere, meaning evapotranspiration is the main pathway by which water returns from the land store to the atmospheric store, sustaining the water cycle rather than allowing water to remain permanently on the land. One mark is given for naming the two component processes, one for identifying the two stores connected (land to atmosphere), and one for linking this to future precipitation.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the two separate sources of water vapour that combine to make up this one term.","Consider which store the water vapour is moving out of, and which store it is moving into.","State what evapotranspiration moves, and name the two stores it connects, in a short explanatory sentence."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"Identify two short-term changes to the water or carbon cycle named in the specification.","a":"Any two of: diurnal change, seasonal change of climate, temperature, sunlight or foliage.","sol":"Short-term changes named in the specification include diurnal changes (occurring within a single day) and seasonal changes in climate, temperature, sunlight and foliage (occurring across a year). One mark is given for each of two such changes, correctly named; at this tariff no explanation of their effects is required.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall that this part of the specification distinguishes 'short-term' change from 'long-term' change — think about how short a timescale still counts as short-term here, from its briefest unit to its longest.","Think broadly about the category of condition that can shift within hours as well as change again over the course of a year, rather than a single specific example of it.","Name two separate short-term changes; no explanation of their effects is needed to earn full marks at this tariff."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how temperature and vegetation affect the rate of decomposition in the tropical rainforest.","a":"High, constant temperature speeds up the metabolism of decomposer organisms; abundant vegetation supplies a constant, large litter input — together these produce very rapid decomposition and nutrient cycling.","sol":"In the tropical rainforest, consistently high temperatures throughout the year speed up the metabolic and enzyme activity of decomposer organisms such as bacteria and fungi, so dead organic matter is broken down far faster than in a cooler biome. Dense vegetation cover also supplies a constant, large volume of leaf litter and other organic matter to the forest floor, giving decomposers an abundant and continuous food supply rather than a seasonal pulse. Together, high temperature and abundant litter input mean nutrients and carbon are recycled through the decomposition pathway extremely quickly, which is why the rainforest's own litter layer stays thin despite the huge volume of organic matter produced by the canopy above it.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what decomposer organisms need in order to work quickly, and what the rainforest's climate and canopy each supply.","Deal with the effect of temperature and the effect of vegetation as two separate linked points, rather than blending them together.","Build each point as cause leading to effect, and note what this fast process means for how much litter actually stays on the forest floor."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Analyse how deforestation for farming has changed flows and stores in the water cycle in one named drainage basin in the tropical rainforest.","a":"In a named Amazon basin/state (e.g. Rondônia), removing trees cuts interception and transpiration, so more rainfall reaches the ground and runs off overland rather than infiltrating, increasing peak discharge and flood risk.","sol":"In drainage basins of the Brazilian Amazon, such as those in Rondônia where forest has been cleared along ‘fishbone’ road patterns for cattle ranching and farming, removing the forest canopy sharply reduces interception, so far more rainfall reaches the ground directly rather than being caught, evaporated or channelled slowly down tree trunks. With fewer roots to hold and aerate the soil and less shade to slow evaporation, infiltration rates fall and a greater proportion of rainfall becomes overland flow. This speeds up the basin's response to storm rainfall, raising peak river discharge and flood risk, while the store of water held in vegetation and topsoil falls because there is less canopy to intercept and transpire it. The basin's water cycle therefore shifts from one dominated by slow, vegetated pathways to one dominated by rapid surface runoff.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which stage of the water cycle depends directly on the tree canopy being present.","Trace what happens to rainfall once that stage is removed, following it through to how quickly water reaches a river channel.","Name the drainage basin or region your answer is based on, then build a chain from canopy loss through to the effect on river discharge."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"A perennial snow patch on Arctic tundra — one that does not fully melt away each summer, so its mass can be compared from one year to the next — had winter accumulation equivalent to 320 mm of water and summer ablation (melt) equivalent to 210 mm of water this year. (a) Calculate the net mass balance for the year. (b) State whether the patch is gaining or losing mass, and outline what this could mean for the permafrost carbon store beneath it.","a":"Net balance = +110 mm w.e. (accumulation exceeds ablation), a positive/gaining balance; continued snow cover for longer could insulate the permafrost and limit further active-layer thaw and carbon release.","sol":"Net mass balance = accumulation − ablation = 320 mm − 210 mm = +110 mm water equivalent for the year. Because accumulation exceeds ablation, the snow patch has a positive balance and is gaining mass overall this year, growing rather than shrinking. A positive balance means the ground beneath it is protected by snow cover for longer, which could keep the active layer shallower, limiting how much previously frozen soil carbon thaws and becomes available for decomposition and release as CO2 or methane; a run of years with a negative balance instead would deepen the active layer and increase carbon release from thawing permafrost.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what ‘mass balance’ compares in a store that both gains and loses water through the year.","Work out which of the two figures given represents what was added to the store and which represents what was lost, then combine them.","State a single net figure with the correct sign and unit, then explain briefly what a positive or negative result implies for the permafrost beneath."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"A tributary draining part of the Amazon rainforest has a channel width of 15 m, a mean depth of 3 m and a mean velocity of 0.5 m/s. Calculate its discharge in cumecs, then convert this figure to litres per second.","a":"Discharge = 15 × 3 × 0.5 = 22.5 cumecs (m³/s); 22.5 m³/s = 22,500 litres per second (1 m³ = 1,000 litres).","sol":"Discharge = cross-sectional area × mean velocity. Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth = 15 m × 3 m = 45 m². Discharge = 45 m² × 0.5 m/s = 22.5 cumecs (m³ per second). To convert to litres per second, multiply by 1,000 (since 1 m³ = 1,000 litres): 22.5 × 1,000 = 22,500 litres per second.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall what discharge measures about a river channel, and which two channel measurements combine to give its cross-sectional area.","Work out the cross-sectional area first, then bring in the velocity figure.","Give your discharge in cumecs first, then convert that single figure into litres per second, showing the conversion factor you used."],"codes":["1.c","2.a"]},{"q":"Assess how effective afforestation and improved agricultural techniques are as strategies to protect the water and carbon cycles in the tropical rainforest, and reach a judgement on their overall effectiveness.","a":"Afforestation restores interception, transpiration and carbon sequestration; improved agriculture (e.g. agroforestry) reduces soil exposure and maintains some canopy — both help, but recovery is slow and effectiveness relies on enforcement and funding.","sol":"Afforestation restores tree cover, rebuilding interception and transpiration so that overland flow and flood risk fall back towards pre-clearance levels, while new tree growth draws carbon back out of the atmosphere and rebuilds the vegetation carbon store lost to deforestation. Improved agricultural techniques, such as agroforestry that keeps some tree cover among crops, reduce the amount of bare soil exposed to rainfall and sun, limiting the rapid decomposition and carbon release that follows full clearance while still allowing farming to continue. Both strategies are genuinely effective at slowing further loss and beginning recovery of the cycles. However, their effectiveness is limited by time — newly planted trees take decades to intercept and store carbon at the rate mature rainforest did — and by how consistently they are enforced and funded, since farmers may revert to full clearance if agroforestry yields less than conventional farming. Overall, both strategies move the water and carbon cycles back towards their pre-disturbance state, but neither restores them quickly, so their effectiveness depends heavily on sustained investment and enforcement over a long period.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which part of the rainforest's water and carbon cycle each strategy directly restores.","Weigh what each strategy achieves against how long it takes, or how reliably it is kept up, to reach a balanced view.","End with an overall judgement on how effective the two strategies are together, not a simple description of what each one does."],"codes":["2.a","4.b"]},{"q":"Explain how the developing oil and gas industry has disturbed the carbon cycle in the Arctic tundra, and outline one strategy used to moderate this impact.","a":"Extraction and infrastructure (e.g. Prudhoe Bay, Alaska) release stored carbon and can warm and disturb the ground, deepening the active layer and releasing further carbon; moderation strategies include elevating pipelines to limit ground disturbance.","sol":"Extracting and burning oil and gas releases carbon that had been stored underground for millions of years directly into the atmosphere, while the heat and physical disturbance from wells, roads and pipelines — such as at Alaska's Prudhoe Bay field, in production since 1977 — can warm the ground locally and deepen the active layer, exposing previously frozen soil carbon to decomposition and further release as CO2 or methane. One strategy used to moderate this impact is elevating pipelines, such as the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System, above the ground on supports, which reduces heat transfer into the permafrost below and allows wildlife such as caribou to continue moving beneath the pipeline route.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about two separate ways the industry disturbs carbon: what is released by extraction itself, and what its infrastructure does to the ground around it.","Name a specific piece of infrastructure or a named oil and gas development to anchor your explanation.","Cover the disturbance first, then give one named management response as a separate final point."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"Analyse the role of positive and negative feedback loops in disturbing or maintaining dynamic equilibrium in the water and carbon cycles.","a":"Positive feedback amplifies an initial change away from equilibrium (e.g. permafrost thaw releasing methane, driving further warming and thaw); negative feedback dampens change, pushing a system back towards balance (e.g. extra plant growth from higher CO2 absorbing more carbon).","sol":"Dynamic equilibrium exists where a system's inputs and outputs stay roughly balanced over time, so its stores remain broadly stable. Positive feedback loops disturb this balance by amplifying an initial change in the same direction: for example, as permafrost in the Arctic tundra thaws, it releases stored methane and CO2, which adds to atmospheric warming, which in turn causes further permafrost thaw — each stage reinforcing the last and pushing the system further from its original equilibrium. Negative feedback loops instead work to restore balance by dampening a change: for example, higher atmospheric CO2 concentrations can stimulate increased plant growth (the ‘CO2 fertilisation effect’), and this extra vegetation absorbs more carbon through photosynthesis, partly offsetting the original rise in atmospheric CO2 and pulling the system back towards its previous balance. Because these two loop types push in opposite directions, the overall trajectory of a system such as the Arctic carbon cycle depends on which loops currently dominate — and evidence that permafrost carbon release is now outpacing plant carbon uptake suggests positive feedback is currently the stronger of the two forces disturbing this system's equilibrium.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what separates a feedback loop that pushes a system further from balance from one that pulls it back towards balance.","Choose one clear named example of each loop type from the water or carbon cycle, tracing each through more than one stage.","Finish by weighing which of the two loop types is currently having the stronger effect on the system you have discussed."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Deforestation (e.g. in Rondônia, Brazil) has become a powerful, rapid control on carbon flows and stores, but the underlying physical controls (temperature, rainfall) still govern the natural rates of photosynthesis and decomposition that make the biome significant — so human activity now dominates the rate of change, though it has not replaced the physical basis of the system.","sol":"Human activity has become a powerful control on the tropical rainforest's carbon cycle, most visibly through deforestation. In Rondônia, in the Brazilian Amazon, clearance for cattle ranching and farming along the ‘fishbone’ pattern of roads removes vegetation that would otherwise photosynthesise and store carbon, while exposing soil organic matter to rapid decomposition in the tropical heat; Brazil's national monitoring recorded approximately 11,000 km² of Amazon deforestation in the year to July 2020 alone, a rate of change no natural process in the region approaches. This human-driven loss converts a long-term carbon store into a source of emissions far faster than natural disturbances such as wildfire or windthrow could. However, the underlying physical controls have not been replaced. The same high, constant temperatures and heavy rainfall that make deforestation so damaging also drive the rainforest's naturally rapid rates of photosynthesis, decomposition and nutrient cycling — the reason an undisturbed rainforest stores and cycles so much carbon in the first place. Where clearance stops, these physical conditions allow carbon cycling to resume quickly through natural regrowth, showing that physical factors still set the baseline the human disturbance acts upon. On balance, human activity is now the main control on the rate and direction of recent change in the Amazon's carbon cycle, since deforestation currently outpaces any natural process of carbon release or recovery; but it operates by disturbing physical processes that remain the underlying reason this biome is such a significant carbon store, so physical factors have not been replaced as the foundation of the system, only overridden in the short term by the scale of human disturbance.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what ‘main control’ would mean here — which factor is currently driving the fastest rate of change, not just which factor is present.","Set out the case for human activity with a named, dated example, then weigh it against what the physical conditions of the biome still explain.","End with a qualified judgement that states how far you agree, rather than a simple yes or no."],"codes":["2.a","3.a","4.a"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of global management strategies in protecting the water and carbon cycles.","a":"Strategies such as afforestation, wetland restoration, carbon trading, international agreements and drainage basin planning genuinely slow disturbance and rebuild stores, but effectiveness varies by strategy, is often slow to take effect, and depends on cooperation that is not guaranteed — so overall effectiveness is real but partial and uneven.","sol":"Global strategies to protect the carbon cycle, such as afforestation and wetland restoration, are effective in principle because they directly rebuild natural carbon stores that human activity has depleted, while reducing emissions through mechanisms such as carbon trading gives businesses and countries a financial incentive to cut fossil fuel combustion rather than relying on voluntary action alone. International agreements extend this by coordinating action across countries, which matters because carbon and water move across national borders regardless of where they are managed. Global water cycle strategies, such as drainage basin planning that manages run-off, surface stores and groundwater together, and fairer water allocation between domestic, industrial and agricultural users, similarly aim to prevent any one use from depleting a shared store faster than it is replenished. These strategies have real, measurable successes: reforestation schemes have restored carbon sequestration capacity in specific regions, and international agreements have set binding targets that would not exist otherwise. However, effectiveness is uneven. Afforestation takes decades to sequester carbon at the rate mature forest once did, so it addresses future flows more than it reverses current stores quickly; carbon trading and international agreements depend on consistent enforcement and on every major emitter participating, and a single large economy withdrawing can undermine the whole scheme's impact; and drainage basin planning requires cooperation between users and, in transboundary basins, between countries with competing interests in the same water. Overall, global management strategies are effective at slowing further disturbance and beginning to rebuild stores, and are more effective the more consistently they are enforced and the more countries commit to them together, but none of them restores the cycles quickly or guarantees results, so their effectiveness should be judged as real but partial rather than complete.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what each type of strategy is trying to restore or protect, for the carbon cycle and for the water cycle separately.","Weigh genuine successes of these strategies against what limits how quickly or completely they work.","Reach an overall judgement on how far these strategies succeed, rather than judging each strategy in isolation."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"Compare the physical factors affecting the water and carbon cycles in the tropical rainforest with those in the Arctic tundra.","a":"Both are affected by temperature, rock permeability/porosity and relief, but the rainforest's high, constant temperatures and rainfall drive fast flows and decomposition, while the tundra's low temperatures and permafrost slow flows and decomposition, building up frozen carbon and water stores instead.","sol":"Both biomes are shaped by the same three physical factors named in the specification — temperature, rock permeability and porosity, and relief — but these factors act in almost opposite ways in each case. In the tropical rainforest, consistently high temperatures and heavy rainfall drive rapid evapotranspiration, fast infiltration through porous, weathered tropical soils, and very fast decomposition, so water and carbon cycle through the system quickly and stores such as the litter layer stay thin. In the Arctic tundra, low temperatures instead slow evapotranspiration and decomposition dramatically, while permafrost beneath the active layer acts as an impermeable barrier regardless of the rock's natural porosity, restricting infiltration and groundwater flow to a shallow surface zone; this combination means organic matter and water accumulate rather than cycling quickly, building a large frozen carbon store below ground rather than a fast-cycling store above it. Relief affects both biomes similarly in principle — steeper relief speeds up runoff and limits infiltration in both — but its practical effect is far smaller in the tundra, where permafrost already controls infiltration regardless of slope. Overall, the same physical factors operate in both biomes, but temperature is the dominant control in each case, and it produces contrasting outcomes: rapid cycling and thin stores in the rainforest, against slow cycling and large frozen stores in the tundra.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the three physical factors both case studies share, then think about how differently each one behaves in a hot biome compared with a cold one.","Build your comparison factor by factor, contrasting the rainforest and the tundra directly rather than describing each case study separately.","End with a statement of which factor matters most in each biome, and what contrasting outcome for stores and flows this produces."],"codes":["2.a","2.b"]},{"q":"A climate graph for an Arctic tundra weather station shows mean monthly temperature ranging from about −28°C in January to 6°C in July, and total annual precipitation of 240 mm, with the wettest month (August) recording 35 mm. (a) Read off and state the station's approximate annual temperature range. (b) Using the shape of this climate graph, explain one reason why the water cycle at this site is dominated by snow rather than rainfall as an input.","a":"(a) Annual range ≈ 34°C (6 − (−28) = 34°C). (b) Mean temperature stays below 0°C for most of the year, only briefly rising above freezing around the short summer peak, so precipitation falling across most months arrives and remains as snow rather than as rain.","sol":"Reading a climate graph means taking specific values directly off the temperature line and precipitation bars rather than estimating. (a) The annual temperature range is the difference between the highest and lowest mean monthly temperatures shown: 6°C (July) − (−28°C, January) = 34°C. (b) The graph's shape — a temperature line that stays below freezing for most months and rises above 0°C only briefly around the summer peak — shows that precipitation falling across most of the year arrives as snow, since air temperatures are too low for rain to occur or to remain liquid on the surface; this is why the tundra's water cycle is fed mainly by snow accumulation and a short summer ablation (melt) season rather than by rainfall runoff, unlike a temperate or tropical climate graph where the temperature line stays above freezing all year.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out the temperature range first by reading the highest and lowest points directly from the graph, then think about what a temperature line mostly below freezing implies for the state precipitation falls in.","Consider how many months of the year the temperature line sits below 0°C, and what that means for whether precipitation stays solid or turns to liquid water.","Give your range as a single number with its unit, then link the overall shape of the graph — not just one figure from it — to the form the cycle's main input takes here."],"codes":["1.c","2.b"]},{"q":"A student investigating a small stream measured its velocity at five equally spaced points across the channel using a flow meter, recording 0.28, 0.35, 0.40, 0.33 and 0.24 m/s. (a) Calculate the mean velocity from this field data. (b) Suggest one appropriate way to present this set of readings, and explain why it suits data collected at points across a channel.","a":"(a) Mean = (0.28 + 0.35 + 0.40 + 0.33 + 0.24) ÷ 5 = 1.60 ÷ 5 = 0.32 m/s. (b) A bar chart or line graph plotting velocity against distance across the channel, because it shows how velocity varies from bank to bank (typically fastest near the centre/surface) rather than hiding that pattern inside a single averaged figure.","sol":"(a) To find the mean, sum the five readings (0.28 + 0.35 + 0.40 + 0.33 + 0.24 = 1.60 m/s) and divide by the number of readings (5), giving a mean velocity of 0.32 m/s. (b) Because the readings were taken at known points across the channel, a graph that plots velocity on one axis against distance from the bank on the other — a bar chart or line graph — is the most appropriate presentation technique, since it preserves the spatial pattern of the data (typically showing velocity increasing towards the centre and surface of the channel, away from frictional drag at the bed and banks), which a single averaged figure or a table alone would obscure. This kind of field data underpins the discharge calculations used elsewhere in this topic, since mean velocity measured this way is exactly the figure used in discharge = cross-sectional area × mean velocity.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Add the five readings together first, then divide by how many readings you have to find the mean.","Think about what these five numbers actually represent — measurements taken at different positions — and what kind of presentation keeps that positional information visible.","Give one clear numerical answer for the mean, then name one presentation method and give a single reason it fits data collected at points across a space, not just any data."],"codes":["1.c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is stored in the cryosphere?","a":"Frozen water, held as ice, snow and permafrost."},{"q":"Name one input to the water cycle.","a":"Any one of precipitation (an input to a water store) or snowmelt/ablation (an output from a snow or ice store that acts as an input to a catchment's liquid water store, e.g. as meltwater feeding a river or groundwater)."},{"q":"Is a small drainage basin an open or a closed system?","a":"Open — water and matter cross its boundary as inputs (precipitation) and outputs (discharge, evapotranspiration)."},{"q":"What does permafrost do to infiltration in the Arctic tundra?","a":"It blocks it, restricting water to a shallow active layer at the surface."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Open system","back":"A system in which both matter and energy cross the boundary, e.g. a single drainage basin gaining water as precipitation and losing it as discharge and evapotranspiration.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Closed system","back":"A system in which energy but not matter crosses the boundary, e.g. the global water cycle, where the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Major water cycle stores","back":"Atmosphere, oceans, water bodies (rivers/lakes), ice (cryosphere), soil, vegetation and groundwater.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Ablation","back":"The loss of ice or snow through melting or sublimation — an output from a snow or ice store, but an input to a catchment's liquid water store as meltwater feeding rivers and groundwater.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Catchment hydrology terms","back":"Infiltration (water entering soil), percolation (water moving down through soil/rock), throughflow (lateral movement through soil) and groundwater flow (movement through saturated rock).","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Photosynthesis","back":"6CO2 + 6H2O + light energy → C6H12O6 + 6O2; removes carbon from the atmosphere and stores it in plant tissue.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Amazon basin case study","back":"Covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine countries; stores an estimated 150–200 billion tonnes of carbon; around half its rainfall is recycled by the forest's own evapotranspiration.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Deforestation impact (rainforest)","back":"Removing trees cuts interception and transpiration, increasing overland flow and flood risk, while exposing soil carbon to faster decomposition and release.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Active layer","back":"The surface layer of tundra soil that thaws each summer and refreezes each winter, sitting above the permanently frozen permafrost.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Prudhoe Bay","back":"Alaska's largest oil field, in production since 1977 and linked to the coast by the roughly 1,300 km Trans-Alaska Pipeline System — a named example of the oil and gas industry's impact on the Arctic tundra.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Dynamic equilibrium","back":"A long-term balance in a system where inputs and outputs are roughly equal, so stores stay broadly stable despite short-term fluctuations.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Positive feedback loop","back":"A change that triggers further change in the same direction, amplifying the original disturbance, e.g. permafrost thaw releasing methane that increases warming, causing more thaw.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Short-term vs long-term change","back":"Short-term changes include diurnal and seasonal shifts in temperature, sunlight and foliage; long-term changes occur over millions of years and alter stores and flows permanently.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Link between the cycles","back":"The two cycles connect through oceans, atmosphere, cryosphere and vegetation — e.g. warmer oceans hold less dissolved CO2 but drive more evaporation in the water cycle.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Global carbon management strategies","back":"Afforestation, wetland restoration, improved agricultural practices and reducing emissions through carbon trading and international agreements.","code":"4.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: ‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","Human activity is now the dominant control on the pace of change in the tropical rainforest's carbon cycle, which is shown most clearly by how far policy alone can move the deforestation rate. Brazil's Action Plan for the Prevention and Control of Deforestation in the Legal Amazon (PPCDAm), launched in 2004, was followed by a sustained fall in the annual deforestation rate through the following decade — a swing in the carbon cycle's main source of disturbance driven by enforcement and land-use policy rather than by any change in climate or vegetation. This shows that human decisions, not physical conditions, currently set the rate at which carbon leaves this store. However, the underlying physical controls have not been displaced: the same high, constant temperatures and heavy year-round rainfall that make cleared land vulnerable to rapid carbon loss also drive the naturally fast photosynthesis and decomposition that let an undisturbed rainforest regenerate its carbon store once clearance pressure eases, as the post-2004 slowdown itself demonstrates. Human activity — expressed through policy as much as through clearance — is therefore the main control on the current rate and direction of change in the Amazon's carbon cycle, but it operates only by speeding up or slowing down physical processes that remain the reason this biome cycles and stores carbon so quickly in the first place."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: ‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","Humans affect the carbon cycle in the rainforest because they cut down trees. This is bad because trees store carbon, so if you cut them down the carbon cycle is disturbed. In Brazil a lot of the rainforest has been cut down for farming. This releases carbon into the air instead of it staying stored in the trees. Deforestation also affects the soil because there are no trees to protect it. So human activity does affect the carbon cycle in the rainforest a lot, mainly through cutting down trees for farming."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the tropical rainforest (Amazon basin)","items":["Location: the Amazon basin covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine South American countries, roughly 60% of it within Brazil.","Climate: hot and wet all year, with mean temperatures of approximately 26–28°C and annual rainfall typically above 2,000 mm.","River: the Amazon River has the largest discharge of any river on Earth, averaging approximately 209,000 cumecs (m³/s) into the Atlantic Ocean.","Moisture recycling: an estimated 50% or more of rainfall over the Amazon is generated by the forest's own evapotranspiration, sometimes called ‘flying rivers’.","Carbon store: the Amazon's vegetation and soil are estimated to store approximately 150–200 billion tonnes of carbon.","Deforestation: Brazil's national space research agency (INPE) recorded approximately 11,000 km² of Amazon deforestation in the year to July 2020.","Named example: Rondônia state, Brazil, shows a ‘fishbone’ pattern of deforestation for cattle ranching and farming spreading out from access roads.","Management: Brazil's Action Plan for the Prevention and Control of Deforestation in the Legal Amazon (PPCDAm), launched in 2004, helped cut the annual deforestation rate through the following decade; REDD+ is an international scheme that pays to reduce emissions from deforestation and forest degradation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Arctic tundra","items":["Location: Arctic tundra covers northern regions including Alaska (USA), northern Canada, Siberia and northern Scandinavia; the North Slope of Alaska is a widely studied example.","Climate: long, severe winters with mean temperatures often below −30°C and a short summer growing season; annual precipitation is low, typically around 250 mm, classing the tundra as a cold desert.","Permafrost: ground that has remained frozen for at least two consecutive years lies beneath a surface ‘active layer’, typically around 0.3–1 m deep, which thaws each summer and refreezes each winter.","Carbon store: Arctic permafrost is estimated to store around 1,500 billion tonnes of carbon — roughly twice the amount currently held in the atmosphere.","Vegetation: low-growing mosses, lichens, sedges and dwarf shrubs give the tundra low net primary productivity, which, combined with cold, slow decomposition, builds up large stores of organic carbon in the soil.","Oil and gas: Prudhoe Bay, on Alaska's North Slope, is the largest oil field in North America, in production since 1977.","Infrastructure: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System (TAPS) runs approximately 1,300 km (800 miles) from Prudhoe Bay to the port of Valdez.","Management: sections of the pipeline are raised on supports above the ground to reduce heat transfer into the permafrost and to allow caribou migration to continue beneath it."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How far are the water and carbon cycles linked? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: ‘water and carbon cycles’ means both systems must appear in every paragraph, not just one; ‘how far’ means the answer must reach a qualified judgement about the strength of the link, not simply list connections; ‘linked’ points to a shared store or flow, not just a general similarity.","Paragraph 1 (establish the strongest link): explain how the cryosphere links the two cycles by locking up both frozen water and frozen carbon together in permafrost, and what happens to both stores at once when permafrost thaws.","Paragraph 2 (develop a second, different link): explain how the oceans link the two cycles by absorbing and releasing both CO2 and water vapour, and how a change driven by one cycle (such as ocean warming) affects the other.","Paragraph 3 (test the limits of the link): explain a case where the two cycles can change largely independently of each other — for example, water extraction from an aquifer having little direct effect on carbon stores — to show the link is not absolute.","Evaluation thread: throughout, weigh how directly each mechanism connects the two cycles (a shared store, acting on both at once) against cases that mainly affect one cycle with only an indirect knock-on effect on the other.","Conclusion must: state a qualified judgement — the two cycles are linked closely and directly through specific shared stores such as the cryosphere and the oceans, but not every change in one cycle has a significant effect on the other, so ‘linked’ is a matter of degree depending on the mechanism involved."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T06:57:50.979Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"No answer key is wrong — I re-solved every calculation independently (mass balance 320 − 210 = +110 mm w.e.; discharge 15 × 3 × 0.5 = 22.5 cumecs = 22,500 l/s; and the four lesson examples, including P = Q + ET ± ΔS giving ΔS = +50 mm) and all agree with their keys — and the real-world figures hold up (INPE's ~11,000 km² for the year to July 2020 against the recorded 10,851 km²; Amazon ~6.7 million km² and ~209,000 cumecs; Prudhoe Bay in production since 1977; TAPS ~1,300 km; permafrost ~1,500 Gt C, which is nearer 1.7× the atmosphere's ~880 Gt C than the \"roughly twice\" claimed, but that is the standard textbook phrasing); the family rules are met in full — two model paragraphs correctly labelled Level 4 and Level 2 and genuinely at those levels, a fact file for each of the two required case studies, an essay plan in the board's command words, and all nine lesson sections closing with what a top-level answer does — so this is close to publishable, but fix the give-away hint at question 3 and the condensation/ablation input-output muddle before it goes to students, and be aware that the two snow-balance scenarios (accumulation exceeding ablation in tundra, and the reverse in the lesson example) are arithmetically sound but physically implausible as stated for a site with no year-to-year snow carryover, and that the Level 4 model paragraph is near-verbatim the solution to question 11."}}}} };
